# 1091. Shortest Path in Binary Matrix

- **LeetCode:** https://leetcode.com/problems/shortest-path-in-binary-matrix/
- **Topic:** Graphs, BFS, Bidirectional BFS, Shortest Path
- **Language:** Java
- **Status:** Learning

## 1. My Intuition

We can solve this problem using BFS because every movement has the same cost.

We can move in 8 directions: horizontally, vertically, and diagonally.

Initially, I considered normal BFS from the top-left cell to the bottom-right cell.

Then I explored Bidirectional BFS, which starts searching from both the source and destination simultaneously.

## 2. My Approach

1. Check whether the source or destination cell is blocked. If so, return -1.
2. Maintain two queues:
   - One starting from the top-left cell.
   - Another starting from the bottom-right cell.
3. Maintain two distance matrices to track distances from both ends.
4. Expand the smaller queue to reduce the search space.
5. Explore all 8 possible directions.
6. If a neighboring cell has already been visited by the opposite BFS, both searches have met.
7. Calculate the shortest path using the distances from both sides.
8. If the searches cannot connect, return -1.

## 3. Where I Got Stuck / My Mistake

Initially, I thought the order of exploring the eight directions might affect the shortest path.

I also needed to understand how both BFS searches identify their meeting point and calculate the final distance.

## 4. What Clicked

Bidirectional BFS reduces the search space by exploring from both ends.

Instead of exploring the entire distance from one side, both searches move towards each other.

The smaller frontier is expanded at each step.

When the searches meet, the combined distances give the shortest path length.

## 5. Final Submitted Code

class Solution {
    public int shortestPathBinaryMatrix(int[][] grid) {
        int n = grid.length;

        if (grid[0][0] == 1 || grid[n - 1][n - 1] == 1) {
            return -1;
        }

        if (n == 1) {
            return 1;
        }

        Queue<int[]> q1 = new LinkedList<>();
        Queue<int[]> q2 = new LinkedList<>();

        int[][] d1 = new int[n][n];
        int[][] d2 = new int[n][n];

        q1.offer(new int[]{0, 0});
        q2.offer(new int[]{n - 1, n - 1});

        d1[0][0] = 1;
        d2[n - 1][n - 1] = 1;

        int[] dr = {-1, 1, 0, 0, -1, -1, 1, 1};
        int[] dc = {0, 0, -1, 1, -1, 1, -1, 1};

        while (!q1.isEmpty() && !q2.isEmpty()) {
            if (q1.size() > q2.size()) {
                Queue<int[]> temp = q1;
                q1 = q2;
                q2 = temp;

                int[][] t = d1;
                d1 = d2;
                d2 = t;
            }

            int size = q1.size();

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                int[] curr = q1.poll();

                int r = curr[0];
                int c = curr[1];

                for (int k = 0; k < 8; k++) {
                    int nr = r + dr[k];
                    int nc = c + dc[k];

                    if (nr < 0 || nr >= n || nc < 0 || nc >= n ||
                        grid[nr][nc] == 1 || d1[nr][nc] != 0) {
                        continue;
                    }

                    if (d2[nr][nc] != 0) {
                        return d1[r][c] + d2[nr][nc];
                    }

                    d1[nr][nc] = d1[r][c] + 1;
                    q1.offer(new int[]{nr, nc});
                }
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
}

## 6. Complexity Analysis

Let N be the number of rows (and columns).

- **Time Complexity:** O(N²)
- **Space Complexity:** O(N²)

Each cell is visited at most once by each BFS search.

## 7. Revision Notes

- Single-source BFS explores from one endpoint.
- Bidirectional BFS explores from both endpoints.
- Use two queues and two distance matrices.
- Explore all eight directions.
- Expand the smaller frontier.
- Combine distances when both searches meet.
